.TH DEX\-WAIT\-HDMI 1 "August 19, 2026" "dexd 0.1.0" "dexd manual"
.SH NAME
dex\-wait\-hdmi \- wait for a connected display before the player starts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dex\-wait\-hdmi
.PP
.BI DEX_HDMI_TIMEOUT= seconds
.B dex\-wait\-hdmi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dex\-wait\-hdmi
polls
.I /sys/class/drm/card*\-HDMI\-A\-*/status
once a second and exits 0 as soon as one connector reads
.BR connected .
The
.B dexd.service
unit runs it before the player
.RB ( ExecStartPre
in the unit), so the player starts only after the wait succeeds.
.PP
A projector can take 30 to 90 seconds to present its EDID while a Raspberry Pi
boots in about 15. With no EDID to read, the Raspberry Pi picks a fallback mode,
typically 1024x768. It does not change mode when the EDID arrives later, so the
installation shows the wrong resolution until someone notices.
.SH OPTIONS
.B dex\-wait\-hdmi
takes no options and no arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/dex\-wait\-hdmi
The script, installed by the dexd package, mode 755.
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm/card*\-HDMI\-A\-*/status
The connector state the script reads. A connector with a display attached reads
.BR connected .
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/dexd.service
The unit that runs the script, in its
.B ExecStartPre
line.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt
The kernel command line that carries the forced display mode and, if one is
stored, the name of the saved EDID file.
.TP
.I /lib/firmware/edid/dex.bin
The saved copy of the display's EDID, when the installation uses one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A connector read
.BR connected ,
either on the first poll or within the wait. The player starts.
.TP
.B 1
No connector reported a display within the wait. The unit fails, so the service
manager restarts it
.RB ( Restart=always
in the unit) and the whole sequence runs again, rather than starting the player
into a display that is not there.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B dex\-wait\-hdmi: no connected HDMI connector after 120s
.B dex\-wait\-hdmi
prints this to standard error and exits 1. The number is the wait in seconds,
from
.BR DEX_HDMI_TIMEOUT .
Check the cable, the display's power and its input. If the display needs longer
than the wait allows, raise
.B DEX_HDMI_TIMEOUT
and
.B TimeoutStartSec=
together, as described under ENVIRONMENT below.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DEX_HDMI_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait. Default 120. The unit sets
.BR TimeoutStartSec=150 ,
above Debian's 90\-second default, so the full wait can finish. Raise
.B TimeoutStartSec=
whenever this value is raised.
.PP
Setting
.B DEX_HDMI_TIMEOUT
on the command line, as in the synopsis, changes one run only. The service reads
it from an override file. Run
.B "sudo systemctl edit dexd"
and put these lines in the editor:
.PP
.EX
[Service]
Environment=DEX_HDMI_TIMEOUT=180
TimeoutStartSec=210
.EE
.PP
.B systemctl edit
reloads the unit, so
.B "systemctl daemon\-reload"
is not needed.
.SH NOTES
The wait is the second safeguard. The first is a forced display mode. A
.B kms_force
value ending in
.B D
makes the connector read
.B connected
before a display is attached. The Raspberry Pi then sets the intended mode at
boot, and the projector locks on when it finishes warming up.
.BR dex\-exhibit\-apply (1)
writes that value into
.IR /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt .
.PP
The wait is the fallback where no forced display mode is set. It also covers a
swapped cable or a replaced projector where one is set.
.PP
A saved copy of the display's EDID is an optional step at install time. Capture
it once while the display is awake:
.PP
.EX
sudo mkdir \-p /lib/firmware/edid
cat /sys/class/drm/card*\-HDMI\-A\-1/edid \e
  | sudo tee /lib/firmware/edid/dex.bin > /dev/null
.EE
.PP
Then open
.I /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt
in an editor
.RB ( "sudo nano /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt" )
and add this to its single line, separated by a space:
.PP
.EX
drm.edid_firmware=HDMI\-A\-1:edid/dex.bin
.EE
.PP
Reboot, then check the line took effect with
.BR "cat /proc/cmdline" .
Use the connector the display is plugged into:
.B HDMI\-A\-2
in both places if it is the second port.
.PP
.BR dex\-exhibit\-apply (1)
leaves
.B drm.edid_firmware
as it is.
.PP
Any HDMI connector satisfies the wait, not only the one the exhibit config
names. The card number is a wildcard
.RB ( card* )
because
.B card0
and
.B card1
swap between kernel versions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dexd (1),
.BR dex\-exhibit\-apply (1),
.BR dex\-sidecar (1)
.PP
